    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Despite the growing number of cyber-attacks per-day, technical attribution of a cyber-attack, or the act of identifying the originating group behind the attack, remains a complex but mission-critical task for defenders. Correct and timely attribution of the adversary shortens cyber defense team response times, better informs mitigation strategies, and equips firms with a better understanding of adversaries' Tactics, Techniques, and Procedures (TTPs) over time.\r\n\r\nThis thesis aims to answer the question: how do LLM-enabled pipelines impact the efficiency and accuracy of cyber-attack attribution? The thesis focuses on two key aspects of the attribution pipeline, (1) the process of extracting TTPs from dense post-incident forensic documentation of the threat actor, and (2) the process of deriving probabilistic estimates of the APT group responsible for the cyber-attack based on high-level TTPs from cyber-incidents the model may not have seen before, emulating the actual task of attributing a new cyber-incident. This thesis tests the accuracy of LLM-generated TTPs by comparing to human-generated baselines and builds out an end-to-end machine learning model that takes in documentation, extracts TTPs using vector embedding search, and uses these TTPs to perform attribution.\r\n\r\nThis study finds that while LLM-enabled pipelines do not recreate human-generated datasets of TTPs with high similarity, these techniques are still capable of capturing the underlying patterns in threat actor behavior and learning from purely behavioral indicators for threat-actor attribution. This study also finds that the performance of the model improves greatly when a human-analyst is added into the feedback loop, providing more information to the LLM as additional details of the case are revealed. In essence, this thesis finds that LLM-enabled pipelines will represent a decision-support tool for analysts in the attribution pipeline.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
